#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/test-all — one command that runs every test suite this repo has.

Author: Timur Isaev

Alloy grew a test for almost everything (the content store, store identity,
the GFX harness, the board gates, the release-bundle proof, the Steam
automation gate) and no single command that ran them. Worse, the one command
that did run automatically — the CI "test" step — exercised a retired
prototype instead of any of them (issue #79). This is that command, and the
registry below is exactly what CI's fast tier gates on.

Tiers
-----
fast  Suites that need nothing this Mac doesn't already have in git and
      Xcode: no cross toolchain, no Wine build tree, no staged release
      runtime. `--tier fast` runs only these, and it is what ci.yml's PR
      gate calls on every push — it has to fit comfortably in the job's
      15-minute budget.
full  fast, plus suites that are slower or more adversarial (seeded stress
      matrices today; once later milestones of #100 land, suites that need
      a local build this Mac deliberately does not keep committed). `--tier
      full` is "everything this machine can run" — see "What this does not
      run" below for what that still excludes.

Adding a suite
---------------
Append one `Suite(...)` to REGISTRY. No crawling, no globs: a "run
everything" tool that discovers scripts will one day execute something that
builds into a shared tree (spikes/WINE-001/work/build-2,
third_party/src/*), which is exactly what TASKS.md warns against. Give it:

  id       stable, kebab-case, never reused for a different suite — it is
           how --only and a red run's summary line name it.
  tier     "fast" (needs only git + Xcode) or "full" (slower, or needs a
           build this repo does not keep committed).
  cmd      argv. Run a shell suite as ("bash", script, *args) so a missing
           +x bit is never mistaken for a probe failure.
  cwd      "." unless the suite genuinely needs another working directory
           (every suite wired so far locates its own package/repo root).
  timeout  generous, but finite — a hang must still fail the run, not hang
           test-all itself.
  probe    a function returning a human-readable reason when the suite
           cannot run here, or None when it can. Build it from
           need_commands(...) / need_paths(...) / every(...) below rather
           than hand-rolling existence checks.

Then bump REGISTRY_SUITE_COUNT below by the same amount. It is pinned by
hand on purpose: a Suite(...) deleted by a bad merge or a careless refactor
would otherwise just make --list print one fewer entry and every verdict
still green, which is the exact silent-drop this registry exists to rule
out. Forgetting the bump is not a quiet mistake either — every run, and
--selftest, refuses with the mismatch instead of passing.

What this does NOT run, and why
---------------------------------
Nothing here touches third_party/src/*, spikes/*/work/, spikes/M12-*, or
runtime/metal12: every suite is an explicit registry entry, never a
filesystem crawl, so those shared and build-artifact trees are never at
risk of being executed by accident.

WINE-001's policy-probe/jit-store suites, CPU-001's corpus and SEH matrix,
and LEGAL-001's bundle/substitution/codec-scan proofs are not wired here.
Every one of them needs a local, uncommitted build — the cross toolchain,
a Wine build tree under spikes/WINE-001/work/build-2, or a staged release
runtime — that a fresh checkout (including hosted CI) does not have, and
that this Mac deliberately does not keep saved inside the project so as to
avoid a much larger and costlier automated build setup. That is a correct,
intentional limit of this tool today, not an oversight: wiring them is
later milestones of issue #100, each gated on its own presence probe (and,
for anything touching build-2, a busy-check so this tool never contends
with the other agent's live run).

Usage
-----
  tools/test-all                  run every wired suite (tier "full")
  tools/test-all --tier fast      run only the zero-toolchain suites
  tools/test-all --only ID ...    run just these suite(s), by id
  tools/test-all --list           print the selected suites and exit
  tools/test-all --json PATH      also write a machine-readable result
  tools/test-all --selftest       prove the engine against suites it makes
                                   up itself; never touches the suites above
"""
